<!-- ============================  UNIT 1  ============================ -->
<div id="unit1" class="page">
  <div class="section-head">Unit 1 — Engineering Polymers</div>

  <!-- Q1 -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q1. Applications of Polymers in Electronics Field</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">4/4 Papers — 100%</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Definition:</strong> Polymers are large molecules made of repeating monomer units having wide-ranging applications in electronics due to their flexibility, low cost, and tunable electrical properties.<br><br>
      <strong>Applications of Polymers in Electronics:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Insulating material:</strong> PVC and rubber are used as wire and cable insulation in electrical systems.</li>
        <li><strong>Printed Circuit Boards (PCB):</strong> Epoxy resin and fiberglass composites (FR4) are used as substrate material in PCBs.</li>
        <li><strong>Semiconductors / Conducting Polymers:</strong> Polyaniline (PANI), Polythiophene are used in organic transistors, OLEDs, and sensors.</li>
        <li><strong>Dielectric material:</strong> Polyimide films are used as dielectric layers in capacitors and microelectronics.</li>
        <li><strong>Encapsulation / Packaging:</strong> Epoxy resins are used to encapsulate IC chips, protecting them from moisture and mechanical damage.</li>
        <li><strong>LCD screens:</strong> Liquid Crystal Polymers (LCP) are used in display technology for flat panel displays.</li>
        <li><strong>Electroluminescent devices / OLED:</strong> PPV (poly para-phenylene vinylene) is used in polymer-based LEDs and OLED displays.</li>
        <li><strong>Energy storage:</strong> Polymer electrolytes are used in solid-state batteries and supercapacitors.</li>
        <li><strong>Solar cells:</strong> Conjugated polymers (P3HT) used in organic photovoltaic cells.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords to Write</div>PVC insulation, Epoxy resin, PCB, Polyimide, PANI (Polyaniline), LCP, PPV, OLED, Encapsulation, Polymer electrolytes, Organic photovoltaics</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div><strong>"I P S D E L E E S"</strong> → Insulation, PCB, Semiconductor/Conducting, Dielectric, Encapsulation, LCD, Electroluminescent, Energy storage, Solar cells</div>
    <div class="warn-block"><div class="ans-label">Common Mistakes</div>Don't just write "PVC is used" — write WHERE exactly. Write material name + application specifically. Mention at least 6-8 points for 4 marks.</div>
    <div class="prob-block"><div class="ans-label">Probability of Appearing Again</div>Near 100% — appeared in ALL 4 previous papers. Direct answer memorization = guaranteed marks.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2 DOP -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q2. Degree of Polymerization — Numerical</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">2 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Formula</div>
      <strong>M.W. of Polymer = M.W. of Monomer × Degree of Polymerization (n)</strong><br><br>
      <strong>Example 1 (Dec 2025 paper):</strong> DOP = 1000000, MW monomer = 28 → MW of Polyethylene = 28 × 1000000 = <strong>28,000,000</strong><br><br>
      <strong>Example 2 (Aug 2025):</strong> MW polymer = 58000, MW monomer = 32 → DOP = 58000/32 = <strong>1812.5</strong><br><br>
      <strong>Example 3 (Jun 2024):</strong> MW polymer = 63750, DOP = 2700 → MW monomer = 63750/2700 = <strong>23.6</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">All Possible Question Forms</div>
      1. Find MW of polymer when DOP and MW of monomer are given → <code>MW poly = MW mono × DOP</code><br>
      2. Find DOP when MW of polymer and monomer are given → <code>DOP = MW poly / MW mono</code><br>
      3. Find MW of monomer when MW of polymer and DOP are given → <code>MW mono = MW poly / DOP</code>
    </div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div>"P = M × n" (Polymer = Monomer × n). Just triangle formula — cover what you want to find.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3 Tg factors -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q3. Factors Affecting Glass Transition Temperature (Tg)</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Definition of Tg:</strong> The temperature at which amorphous polymers undergo transformation from glassy/hard state to rubbery/viscous state is called Glass Transition Temperature.<br><br>
      <strong>Factors Affecting Tg:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Crystallinity:</strong> Higher the crystallinity of polymer, higher is the Tg value. Crystalline regions restrict chain mobility.</li>
        <li><strong>Chain Flexibility:</strong> More flexible polymer chains (with single bonds) have lower Tg. Rigid chains (aromatic rings) have higher Tg.</li>
        <li><strong>Molecular Weight:</strong> Higher the molecular weight, higher is the Tg, because longer chains have more entanglements.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-linking:</strong> Higher the degree of cross-linking, higher is the Tg, because cross-links restrict segmental motion.</li>
        <li><strong>Plasticizers:</strong> Addition of plasticizers lowers the Tg by increasing free volume and reducing intermolecular forces.</li>
        <li><strong>Branching:</strong> More branching → lower Tg due to increased free volume between chains.</li>
        <li><strong>Polarity / Intermolecular forces:</strong> Strong intermolecular forces (polar groups, H-bonding) → higher Tg.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Importance of Tg:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Determines whether polymer is used above Tg (rubbery — like polyisoprene for rubber) or below Tg (glassy — like polystyrene for hard plastics).</li>
        <li>Helps in selecting polymers for specific temperature applications.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Crystallinity, Chain flexibility, Molecular weight, Cross-linking, Plasticizers, Branching, Intermolecular forces, Free volume, Rubbery state, Glassy state</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div><strong>CCMCPBP</strong> → Crystallinity, Chain flexibility, Molecular weight, Cross-linking, Plasticizers, Branching, Polarity</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4 Compounding -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q4. Constituents Added During Compounding of Polymers</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      Compounding is the process of mixing polymers with various additives to improve/modify their properties.<br><br>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Constituent</th><th>Function</th><th>Example</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1. Plasticizers</strong></td><td>Increase flexibility and reduce brittleness; lower Tg</td><td>DOP (Di-octyl phthalate), DBP</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2. Fillers</strong></td><td>Increase hardness, strength; reduce cost</td><td>Carbon black, silica, chalk, clay</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3. Stabilizers</strong></td><td>Prevent degradation due to heat/UV/light</td><td>Lead salts, Tin compounds</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4. Lubricants</strong></td><td>Reduce friction during processing; prevent sticking</td><td>Stearic acid, Paraffin wax</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>5. Colorants / Pigments</strong></td><td>Provide colour to polymer products</td><td>Organic dyes, Titanium dioxide (white)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>6. Flame Retardants</strong></td><td>Reduce flammability of polymers</td><td>Halogen compounds, Antimony oxide</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>7. Antioxidants</strong></td><td>Prevent oxidative degradation</td><td>BHT (Butylated Hydroxy Toluene)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>8. Blowing Agents</strong></td><td>Create foam/cellular structure (for insulation)</td><td>Azodicarbonamide</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
