  <!-- Q5 Conducting Polymer Doping -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q5. Methods of Doping — Intrinsically Conducting Polymers</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Definition:</strong> Conducting polymers are those polymers which conduct electricity. Intrinsically conducting polymers (ICPs) have conjugated π-electron system in their backbone. Doping increases conductivity by oxidation or reduction.<br><br>
      <strong>Types of Doping:</strong><br><br>
      <strong>A) Chemical Doping:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>p-type doping (Oxidative doping):</strong> Polymer is oxidized by removing electrons. Dopant acts as oxidizing agent (e.g., AsF5, I2, Cl2). Creates positive charge (holes) in polymer chain → increases conductivity. Example: PANI + HCl → conducting emeraldine salt.</li>
        <li><strong>n-type doping (Reductive doping):</strong> Polymer is reduced by adding electrons. Dopant acts as reducing agent (e.g., Na, K, Li). Creates negative charge in polymer → increases conductivity.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>B) Electrochemical Doping:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Polymer film is used as electrode in electrochemical cell.</li>
        <li>p-doping: polymer film at anode (oxidation).</li>
        <li>n-doping: polymer film at cathode (reduction).</li>
        <li>Advantage: Controllable and reversible doping.</li>
      </ul>
      <strong>C) Photo-doping:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>UV/visible light irradiation causes electron-hole pair generation in the polymer.</li>
        <li>Temporary effect — conductivity returns to original after removal of light.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
