  <div class="section-head">Unit 3 — Corrosion Prevention Techniques</div>

  <!-- Sacrificial anode -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q1. Sacrificial Anode Cathodic Protection — Diagram, Method, Applications</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Principle of Cathodic Protection:</strong> The metal to be protected is forced to behave as cathode, so it does not undergo oxidation/corrosion.<br><br>
      <strong>Method (Sacrificial Anode):</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>The metallic structure to be protected is connected to an active metal (Zn, Al, or Mg) by an insulated wire.</li>
        <li>The more active metal acts as <strong>anode (sacrificial)</strong> and gets corroded, while the main metal (cathode) remains protected.</li>
        <li>The sacrificial anode is placed in <strong>backfill (coal + NaCl)</strong> to increase electrical contact.</li>
        <li>When sacrificial metal is completely consumed, it is replaced by a fresh piece.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Diagram description:</strong> Underground pipeline (cathode) connected via insulated wire to Zinc/Mg block (anode) placed in backfill material.<br><br>
      <strong>Applications:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Buried steel pipelines</li>
        <li>Ship hull (Magnesium/Zinc rods bolted to sides)</li>
        <li>Hot water tanks (Zinc rods inserted)</li>
        <li>Buried cables</li>
        <li>Marine offshore structures</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Sacrificial anode, cathode, backfill (coal + NaCl), Zinc, Magnesium, Aluminum, insulated wire, more active metal, anodic metal</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div>"Sacrifice Zinc to save Iron" — the less valuable metal (Zn/Mg) sacrifices itself to protect the important structure (iron pipe/ship).</div>
    <div class="warn-block"><div class="ans-label">Common Mistake</div>Don't confuse sacrificial anode with impressed current method. Sacrificial = natural galvanic effect, no DC supply needed. Impressed current = external DC supply needed.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Impressed current -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q2. Impressed Current Cathodic Protection — Diagram, Method, Applications</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-med">2/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★☆</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Method:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>An impressed current is applied in <strong>opposite direction</strong> to nullify the corrosion current.</li>
        <li>This converts the corroding metal from anode → cathode.</li>
        <li>Impressed current is derived from a D.C. source.</li>
        <li>D.C. is given to an <strong>insoluble anode</strong> (graphite, stainless steel, or scrap iron) buried in soil.</li>
        <li>The <strong>negative terminal</strong> of D.C. is connected to the pipeline to be protected.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Applications:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Open water box coolers</li>
        <li>Water tanks</li>
        <li>Buried water or gas pipelines</li>
        <li>Condensers</li>
        <li>Transmission line towers</li>
        <li>Marine piers and Laid-up ships</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Impressed current, D.C. source, insoluble anode, graphite, negative terminal, nullify corrosion current, active to cathode</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Anodic vs Cathodic Coating -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q3. Anodic vs Cathodic Coating — Why Anodic is Better</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Metallic Coating Definition:</strong> Application of a thin layer of metal on base metal to protect it from corrosion.<br><br>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Point</th><th>Anodic Coating</th><th>Cathodic Coating</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Position in galvanic series</td><td>Coating metal is HIGHER (more active) than base metal</td><td>Coating metal is LOWER (less active) than base metal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Example</td><td>Zinc/Al/Cr coated on Steel</td><td>Tin coated on Steel; Ag/Au on brass</td></tr>
        <tr><td>When coating ruptures</td><td>Coating metal (anode) corrodes and PROTECTS base metal</td><td>Base metal becomes anode and CORRODES (intense pitting)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reliability</td><td>More reliable</td><td>Less reliable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Method</td><td>Galvanizing process</td><td>Tinning process</td></tr>
      </table>
      <br>
      <strong>Why Anodic Coating is Better:</strong> Even if the coating is ruptured/damaged, the coating metal (Zn/Al) being more active acts as anode and continues to corrode, protecting the base metal (steel). Thus, protection continues even after damage. In cathodic coating, once coating is ruptured, base metal gets exposed and intensely corroded.
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Galvanic series, anodic, cathodic, galvanizing, tinning, pitting, ruptured, more active, less active, sacrificial protection even after rupture</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div>"Anodic = Always protects even after damage (like a selfless guardian). Cathodic = Can only protect when intact (breaks down when damaged)."</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Factors affecting corrosion -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q4. Factors Affecting Rate of Corrosion — Nature of Metal</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Factors Based on Nature of Metal:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Position in Galvanic Series:</strong> More active metal (higher in series) corrodes faster. Greater the separation between two metals in galvanic series, faster the corrosion of anodic metal.</li>
        <li><strong>Relative area of anode and cathode:</strong> Larger cathode area relative to anode → faster and more localized corrosion of anode. Small anode + Large cathode = severe corrosion.</li>
        <li><strong>Purity of Metal:</strong> Impurities form galvanic microcells. If impurity is passive (lower in series), metal corrodes. More impurities → more corrosion.</li>
        <li><strong>Physical State of Metal:</strong> Smaller grain size → greater rate of corrosion (e.g., steel corrodes faster than cast iron). Stressed areas act as anode → more corrosion.</li>
        <li><strong>Nature of Oxide Film:</strong> Protective non-porous oxide film → no further corrosion. Porous oxide film (Fe, Mg, Na) → corrosion continues until complete destruction.</li>
        <li><strong>Over Voltage:</strong> Metals with higher over voltage (Zn, Pb, Cr, Ni) show less corrosion in acidic medium.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Galvanic series, anodic area, cathodic area, grain size, oxide film, porous, non-porous, over voltage, impurities, stressed part</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div><strong>"G A P P N O"</strong> → Galvanic series, Area ratio, Purity, Physical state, Nature of oxide film, Overvoltage</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Galvanizing vs Tinning -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q5. Galvanizing — Definition, Process, Applications</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-high">3/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">2-4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Definition:</strong> Coating of zinc on iron or steel is called galvanizing. It is an anodic coating method.<br><br>
      <strong>Process:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Steel article is cleaned with dil. H₂SO₄ (pickling).</li>
        <li>Washed with water and dried.</li>
        <li>Dipped in bath of molten zinc at <strong>425–450°C</strong>.</li>
        <li>Surface covered with flux like <strong>NH₄Cl</strong> to prevent oxidation.</li>
        <li>After removal, article is rolled to make uniform coating and remove excess zinc.</li>
        <li>Cooled slowly.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Applications:</strong>
      <ul>
