  <!-- Kurtosis -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      <span class="card-title">Q5. Kurtosis — Types, Significance</span>
      <span class="freq-badge freq-med">2/4 Papers</span>
      <span class="marks-tag">4 Marks</span>
      <span class="stars">★★★★☆</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ans-block">
      <div class="ans-label">Full Exam Answer</div>
      <strong>Definition:</strong> Kurtosis measures the shape and peakness of a dataset's frequency distribution, indicating the extent to which data points differ from a normal distribution (outliers). Expected value = 3.<br><br>
      <strong>Types:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Mesokurtic (Normal):</strong> Kurtosis ≈ 0 (or = 3). Normal bell curve. Data follows normal distribution.</li>
        <li><strong>Leptokurtic (Positive):</strong> Kurtosis &gt; 3 (value more than normal). Steep/sharp peak. Heavy tails. Large number of outliers. Example: stock market returns during crisis.</li>
        <li><strong>Platykurtic (Negative):</strong> Kurtosis &lt; 3 (value less than normal). Flat/pale peak. Light tails. Small number of outliers.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Significance:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Describes "fatness" of tails in probability distributions.</li>
        <li>Risk measurement — indicates how often investment price moves dramatically (financial risk analysis).</li>
        <li>Helps identify outliers in a dataset.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Peakness, Mesokurtic, Leptokurtic, Platykurtic, outliers, expected value = 3, heavy tails, fat tails, normal distribution</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div>"MLP" → Meso (normal), Lepto (sharp/high — leap up), Platy (flat — like a platypus). Leptokurtic &gt; 3, Platykurtic &lt; 3.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Definitions Unit 4 -->
  <div class="section-head">Important Definitions — Unit 4</div>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Variance</strong></td><td>A measurement of how far each number in a data set is from the mean. Formula: σ² = Σ(xi-μ)²/N. It is the square of standard deviation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Standard Deviation</strong></td><td>Statistical measure used to calculate the average deviation from the mean. σ = √(Variance). Shows typical deviation from mean.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sampling Error</strong></td><td>Errors related to the nature or size of the sample selected for study. Formula: Sampling Error = Estimated Value – Actual Value.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Non-Sampling Error</strong></td><td>Errors related to the collection of data (not dependent on sample size). Can occur in any kind of survey or study.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Uncertainty</strong></td><td>Uncertainty in statistics is measured by the amount of error in an estimate of the mean or average value of a population.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Residual</strong></td><td>Difference between an observed value and a predicted value in regression analysis. Residual = Observed value – Predicted value.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Calibration</strong></td><td>The comparison of a measurement device against a known standard to ensure it is operating safely and efficiently. It restores accuracy of instruments.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Linear Regression</strong></td><td>Used to predict relationship between two variables by applying a linear equation Y = a + bX, where b is slope and a is intercept.</td></tr>
