      <strong>Definition:</strong> Photovoltaic materials are semiconductor materials that convert solar energy (light energy) into electrical energy through the photovoltaic effect. Materials used: Silicon (mono/poly/amorphous), GaAs, Metal chalcogenides.<br><br>
      <strong>A. Electronic Properties:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Band Gap:</strong> Energy difference between valence band and conduction band. Suitable band gap is crucial for efficient photon absorption. Silicon: 1.1 eV.</li>
        <li><strong>Carrier Mobility:</strong> Ability of charge carriers (electrons/holes) to move through material. Higher mobility → better efficiency.</li>
        <li><strong>Doping:</strong> N-type (phosphorus/arsenic added — extra electrons) and P-type (boron/gallium added — creates holes) for creating p-n junctions.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>B. Optical Properties:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Absorption Spectrum, Band Gap Energy, Quantum Efficiency</li>
        <li>Reflectance and Transmittance, Anti-Reflective Coatings</li>
        <li>Refractive Index, Photoluminescence, Light Trapping</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>C. Transport Properties:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Photo-generation:</strong> Absorption of photons creates electron-hole pairs (excitons).</li>
        <li><strong>Doping:</strong> Creates n-type and p-type regions influencing charge carrier distribution.</li>
        <li><strong>Junctions:</strong> p-n junctions separate charge carriers, creating potential difference for electricity flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Excitation:</strong> Photons excite electrons from valence to conduction band.</li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Advantages:</strong> Renewable energy, no pollution, low maintenance, long life. <br>
      <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> High initial cost, weather dependent, requires large area.
    </div>
