          <ul>
            <li>The portion exposed to light becomes SOLUBLE in developer.</li>
            <li>Unexposed portion remains INSOLUBLE (remains on surface).</li>
            <li>Examples: Phenolic Novolak Resin, PMMA (Polymethyl Methacrylate)</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Negative Photoresist:</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>The portion exposed to light becomes INSOLUBLE (hardened/crosslinked).</li>
            <li>Unexposed portion gets DISSOLVED in developer.</li>
            <li>Examples: Cyclized polyisoprene (rubber)</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <strong>Applications:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li>Micro contact printing (using elastomeric stamp)</li>
        <li>Printed circuit boards (PCB manufacturing)</li>
        <li>Silicon wafer patterning and etching</li>
        <li>Microelectronics — IC fabrication</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="kw-block"><div class="ans-label">Keywords</div>Photolithography, positive resist, negative resist, PMMA, Novolak resin, photoengraving, soluble, insoluble, UV light, developer, IC fabrication, PCB</div>
    <div class="trick-block"><div class="ans-label">Memory Trick</div>Positive resist = "Positive people expose themselves" (exposed area gets dissolved/removed). Negative resist = "Negative people hide when exposed" (exposed area hardens and stays).</div>
  </div>

